  tutorialCard.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend','<h3>Research workflow: source projection on a uniform waveguide port</h3><p>In <b>Guided modes</b>, converge the cross section and open <b>Uniform-port source → guided-mode projection</b>. The analytic source supports elliptical/super-Gaussian waists, offset, rotation, phase, polarization, and tilt. Imported complex Ex/Ey fields give the normalized electric-profile diagnostic; include physically consistent Hx/Hy for forward/backward Lorentz-reciprocity power projection. Compare source, mode, and local overlap-density maps, screen source parameters, then run the half-mesh certificate and vary padding separately. Re-importing a solved mode must give unit forward overlap and negligible backward overlap. <b>This result is not finite-grating or fiber-to-chip efficiency.</b> A finite-device claim requires the grating, substrate, radiation boundaries, transition, and normalized output ports in one Maxwell solution with a closed power budget. Formalism: Snyder &amp; Love, <i>Optical Waveguide Theory</i>; vector modes: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/JLT.2008.923643" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fallahkhair et al. (2008)</a>; finite-coupler context: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1143/JJAP.45.6071" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Taillaert et al. (2006)</a>.</p>');
  const coupledBranchCard=document.createElement('div');coupledBranchCard.className='card';coupledBranchCard.innerHTML=`<div class="eyebrow">Hybrid-mode interpretation</div><h2>Upper/lower coupled-branch composition</h2><p class="hint">Fit two tracked wavelength branches to a 2×2 coupled-oscillator model with one linear bare photonic branch and one constant bare matter energy. The result reports coupling strength, minimum splitting, and Hopfield composition.</p><div class="actions"><button id="useTrackedBranches" class="secondary">Use latest tracked branches</button><button id="downloadBranchTemplate" class="secondary">Download CSV template</button></div><label for="coupledBranchFile">Or import branch CSV</label><input id="coupledBranchFile" type="file" accept=".csv,text/csv"><p class="hint">Columns: parameter,branch_1_um,branch_2_um. Branch labels may swap; the fit sorts upper/lower photon energy at every point.</p><div class="row"><div><label for="coupledCavityWidth">Bare photonic linewidth (meV; optional)</label><input id="coupledCavityWidth" type="number" step="any"></div><div><label for="coupledMatterWidth">Bare matter linewidth (meV; optional)</label><input id="coupledMatterWidth" type="number" step="any"></div></div><div class="actions"><button id="runCoupledBranches" class="primary">Fit coupled branches</button><button id="exportCoupledBranches" class="secondary" disabled>Export result</button></div><p id="coupledBranchStatus" class="notice">Load two branches across at least four parameter values.</p><canvas id="coupledBranchPlot" class="plot" width="1100" height="560" aria-label="Coupled upper and lower branches"></canvas><div id="coupledBranchReport"></div><details><summary>Interpretation boundary</summary><p class="hint">Hopfield fractions are fitted eigenvector composition, not illumination or collection efficiency. A convincing strong-coupling claim also needs stable branches, independently justified bare dispersions and linewidths, convergence, and comparison against an uncoupled or weak-coupling explanation.</p></details>`;document.querySelector('section').append(coupledBranchCard);scrollableChart(document.getElementById('coupledBranchPlot'));let coupledBranchInput=null,coupledBranchResult=null;
